<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP, for three local hosts, in DEBUG BUILDS ONLY.

    This file lives under src/debug and is referenced only from the debug
    manifest, so it is not merged into release or profile builds. A release
    build therefore keeps Android's default: cleartext denied everywhere, on
    every API level from 28 onwards.

    WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL

    The Laravel backend runs on the developer's machine over plain HTTP. An
    emulator reaches it at 10.0.2.2, and a physical device reaches it at
    127.0.0.1 through `adb reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000`. Without an exception
    both fail with a cleartext-not-permitted error that names no cause.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE

    No <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">. That is the one-line
    version of this file and it permits cleartext to EVERY host, including
    whatever a misconfigured build might point at. The exception is granted per
    domain instead, so a typo in RIDEMATE_API_BASE_URL fails loudly rather than
    silently sending a passcode over an unencrypted connection to somewhere
    unintended.

    No LAN address or range. Reaching a device over the local network is
    possible but needs a host that changes with the network, and baking one in
    would mean permitting a range that belongs to whatever café the laptop is
    sitting in. `adb reverse` needs no such permission and works anywhere.

    includeSubdomains is false on all three: these are exact hosts, and
    nothing about them has subdomains worth trusting.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The host machine, as seen from the Android emulator. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
        <!-- A physical device, forwarded by `adb reverse`. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
